<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apennines</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Apennine Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Apennines are the mountain range that runs about 1,200 kilometres down the length of the Italian peninsula, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. Their valleys and uplands were home to many ancient Italic peoples, such as the Samnites, Sabines and Umbrians, and their passes linked the Tyrrhenian and Adriatic sides of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The highest peak is Corno Grande in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-sasso">Gran Sasso</a> massif.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apennines.jpg" alt="Apennines" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luciodem · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Il_Gran_Sasso_d%27Italia,_il_paretone_nord.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Apennines are a mountain range made up of parallel smaller chains that extend c. 1,200 km along peninsular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In the northwest they join the Ligurian Alps at Altare, and in the southwest they end at Reggio di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. Since 2000 the Italian Environment Ministry has defined the Apennines System to include the mountains of north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, a total distance of 1,500 km. The range has three sectors, northern, central and southern. The eastern slopes down to the Adriatic Sea are steep, whereas the western slopes form foothills on which most of the towns of peninsular Italy stand. The range has about 21 peaks over 1,900 m, most of them in the central Apennines.</p><p>The Apennines rose from the sea beginning in the early Neogene, about 20 million years ago, and the process continues. They are much younger than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, which were millions of years old by then, and do not share their origin. Compression raised a fold and thrust belt on the Adriatic side, while the crust under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> spread and produced fault-block mountains on the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name is most often derived from the Celtic penn, mountain or summit, which would have been assigned in the 4th century BCE or earlier during the Celtic domination of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and it first applied to the north Apennines. Historical linguists have never found a derivation they all agree on, and none is accepted unquestionably.</p><p>The Apennine culture, named for the range, is a technology complex of central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a>, the 15th and 14th centuries BCE. Its pottery was a burnished ware incised with spirals, meanders and geometrical zones filled with dots or dashes. Its people were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herdsmen who grazed their animals on the mountain meadows of central Italy and lived in small hamlets in defensible places. On the move between summer pastures they built temporary camps or lived in caves and rock shelters. Their pottery has been found on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lipari">Lipari</a>.</p><p>In the north, the Bocchetta di Altare, at 436 m, is the border between the Ligurian Alps and the Ligurian Apennines. It is the main and only feasible overland route between the coastal plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> and the north Italian plain, and defenders of north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> have had to control it since ancient times, as its fortifications show.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Apennines preserve some relatively intact ecosystems, among them some of the best-preserved forests and montane grasslands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, now protected by national parks. They are among the few places in Europe where large predators such as the Italian wolf and the Marsican brown bear still exist. The highest peak, Corno Grande, carries the Calderone glacier, the only glacier in the Apennines.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apennine Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Genoa was an old Ligurian port, squeezed between the <b>Apennines</b> and the sea, which had suffered Muslim raids in the tenth century and by the eleventh was raiding back, joining Pisa in attacks on Sardinia and on Mahdia in 1087.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake originating in the Central <b>Apennines</b> was felt in Rome and Spoleto and damaged the basilica of San Paolo Fuori le Mura.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-29">29 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Agrippa Menenius Lanatus, a consul of the Roman Republic, celebrated a triumph for his victory over the Sabines, an Italic people of the central <b>Apennine Mountains</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aequi lived in the upper valleys of the Aniene, Turano and Salto rivers in the <b>Apennines</b> east of Latium.</blockquote><a href="/state/aequi">Aequi</a></li><li><blockquote>It controlled the Via Emilia and a route across the <b>Apennines</b> to Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-parma">Commune of Parma</a></li><li><blockquote>Pistoia, at the foot of the <b>Apennines</b> between Florence and Lucca, had consuls by 1105.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-pistoia">Commune of Pistoia</a></li></ul></section>
